WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.780
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.466
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:16.343
Unit two: Weather & Environment News. Today you will report forecasts with 'is expected to' and the passive.

4
00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:19.214
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.458
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:25.376
Forecast.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:29.521
Forecast means a statement about what the weather will be like.

8
00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:32.395
The forecast says it will rain tomorrow.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:35.543
Your turn. Say: Forecast.

10
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.506
Forecast.

11
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.054
Climate.

12
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.257
Climate means the usual weather of a place over many years.

13
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.291
Cambodia has a warm, tropical climate.

14
00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:51.733
Your turn. Say: Climate.

15
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:55.185
Climate.

16
00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.532
Natural disaster.

17
00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:02.780
Natural disaster means a sudden event in nature that causes great damage.

18
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:06.497
A natural disaster can destroy homes in minutes.

19
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:10.363
Your turn. Say: Natural disaster.

20
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.724
Natural disaster.

21
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:16.357
Drought.

22
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.174
Drought means a long period with very little rain.

23
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:23.236
The drought left the fields dry and brown.

24
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.560
Your turn. Say: Drought.

25
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.204
Drought.

26
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.684
Flood.

27
00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:35.705
Flood means a large amount of water covering dry land.

28
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:38.792
The flood forced families to leave their homes.

29
00:01:39.383 --> 00:01:42.001
Your turn. Say: Flood.

30
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:45.694
Flood.

31
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.544
Conservation.

32
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.349
Conservation means the protection of nature and natural resources.

33
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.460
Conservation saves forests for the future.

34
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.551
Your turn. Say: Conservation.

35
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.610
Conservation.

36
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.293
Temperature.

37
00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:08.061
Temperature means how hot or cold something is.

38
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.265
The temperature reached thirty-eight degrees.

39
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.584
Your turn. Say: Temperature.

40
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:18.446
Temperature.

41
00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:20.022
Warning.

42
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:23.899
Warning means a message telling people about danger.

43
00:02:24.483 --> 00:02:26.425
The government issued a storm warning.

44
00:02:27.017 --> 00:02:29.704
Your turn. Say: Warning.

45
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:33.475
Warning.

46
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:37.627
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:43.342
Visal, the news says heavy rain is expected on Friday. How do reporters know?

48
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:52.605
They rely on a forecast from weather experts. Reporters write 'is expected to' or 'is forecast to' because nothing is certain yet.

49
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:55.739
What if the rain becomes dangerous?

50
00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:05.449
Then the authorities issue a warning. In the news we read: 'A flood warning was issued for the river towns.'

51
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.666
Why is the passive used so often?

52
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:15.630
The action matters more than the person. 'Thousands were evacuated' tells us the important fact first.

53
00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:18.348
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:22.878
Grammar focus: Passive and 'is expected to' in reports.

55
00:03:23.178 --> 00:03:24.353
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.978
Heavy rain is expected to hit the coast on Friday.

57
00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:31.765
A flood warning was issued for three provinces.

58
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.590
Thousands of people were evacuated before the storm.

59
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:43.723
Use 'is expected to + verb' for predictions and the passive (was issued, were evacuated) when the action is more important than the person.

60
00:03:44.483 --> 00:03:48.215
Be careful! Don't say: Rain is expect to hit Friday.

61
00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:52.735
Say: Rain is expected to hit Friday.

62
00:03:53.035 --> 00:03:55.545
Use the participle 'expected' after is.

63
00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:58.951
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:01.000
Reporting the Weather.

65
00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.517
Weather reporters turn a complex forecast into clear news for the public.

66
00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:11.540
Climate patterns are studied for years, but a daily forecast looks only a few days ahead.

67
00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:17.911
When a natural disaster threatens, a warning is issued and people are told to move to safe places.

68
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.127
Drought and flood stories also remind readers why conservation of land and water matters.

69
00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:29.165
Accurate reporting can therefore protect lives as well as inform.

70
00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:34.257
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:35.117 --> 00:04:37.755
Heavy rain blank to hit the coast on Friday.

72
00:04:38.055 --> 00:04:42.486
A, is expected. B, expects. C, was expect.

73
00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:48.346
The answer is A: is expected.

74
00:04:49.483 --> 00:04:52.238
A long period with very little rain is a blank.

75
00:04:52.538 --> 00:04:55.471
A, flood. B, drought. C, warning.

76
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.592
The answer is B: drought.

77
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:03.808
A message about danger is a blank.

78
00:05:04.108 --> 00:05:07.529
A, forecast. B, warning. C, temperature.

79
00:05:11.067 --> 00:05:12.764
The answer is B: warning.

80
00:05:13.917 --> 00:05:15.638
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:15.938 --> 00:05:21.124
A, A warning issued was.. B, A warning was issued.. C, A warning is issue..

82
00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:27.177
The answer is B: A warning was issued..

83
00:05:28.317 --> 00:05:31.477
According to the reading, accurate reporting can protect…

84
00:05:31.777 --> 00:05:35.640
A, lives. B, tickets. C, newspapers.

85
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.397
The answer is A: lives.

86
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.995
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.173
Why is accurate weather reporting important for public safety?

88
00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:00.646
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:00.946 --> 00:06:23.410
Accurate weather reporting is important because people use it to make safe choices. If a storm is forecast, families can move to higher ground and farmers can protect their crops. A wrong forecast, however, may cause panic or leave people unprepared. For this reason, journalists should always check official sources.

90
00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.408
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:26.317 --> 00:06:31.372
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive and 'is expected to' in reports.

92
00:06:31.672 --> 00:06:35.207
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

93
00:06:35.507 --> 00:06:37.939
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:41.286
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

